====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|heading}} ''Boundary, land within a boundary.''
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[boundary]]; a [[border]] or [[frontier]]. {{defdate|9th–19th c.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A boundary-post or [[fence]]. {{defdate|13th–18th c.}}
## A stone or post used to indicate position and guide travellers. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1859|author=Henry Bull|title=A history, military and municipal, of the ancient borough of the Devizes
|passage=I do remember a great thron in Yatton field near Bristow-way, against which Sir William Waller's men made a great fire and killed it. I think the stump remains, and was a '''mark''' for travellers.}}
## {{lb|en|sports}} The line indicating an athlete's starting-point. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} A type of small region or principality. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Tolkien Two Towers|passage=There dwells Théoden son of Thengel, King of the '''Mark''' of Rohan.}}
## {{lb|en|historical}} A [[common]], or area of common land, especially among early Germanic peoples. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
# {{lb|en|heading}} ''Characteristic, sign, indication for measurement; visible impression.''
## An [[omen]]; a symptomatic indicator of something. {{defdate|from 8th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|text=depend upon it, you will speedily receive from me a letter of thanks for this as well as for every other '''mark''' of your regard during my stay in Hertfordshire.}}
## A characteristic feature. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##: {{uxa|en|A good sense of manners is the '''mark''' of a true gentleman.}}
##* {{RQ:Browne Religio Medici|text=there is surely a physiognomy, which those experienced and master mendicants observe, whereby they instantly discover a merciful aspect, and will single out a face, wherein they spy the signatures and '''marks''' of mercy.}}
## {{sid|en|visual impression, blemish, stain}} A visible impression or sign; a [[blemish]], [[scratch]], or [[stain]], whether [[accidental]] or [[intentional]]. {{defdate|from 9th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Stoker Dracula|text=Then she put before her face her poor crushed hands, which bore on their whiteness the red '''mark''' of the Count's terrible grip{{nb...}}.}}
## A [[sign]] or [[brand]] on a person. {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd|chapter=III.iv.2.6|passage=Doubt not of thine election, it is an immutable decree; a '''mark''' never to be defaced: you have been otherwise, you may and shall be.}}
## A written character or sign. {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
##: {{uxa|en|The font wasn't able to render all the diacritical '''marks''' properly.}}
## A stamp or other indication of provenance, quality etc. {{defdate|from 11th c.}}
##: {{uxa|en|With eggs, you need to check for the quality '''mark''' before you buy.}}
##* {{RQ:Donne Devotions|chapter=13. Prayer|page=329|passage=But vvhat a vvretched, and diſconſolate ''Hermitage'' is that ''Houſe'', vvhich is not viſited by thee {{quote-gloss|God}}, and vvhat a ''VVayue'', and ''Stray'' is that ''Man'', that hath not thy '''''Markes''''' vpon him?}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1876|author=w:Edward H. Knight|title=American Mechanical Dictionary
|passage=The '''mark''' of the artisan is found upon the most ancient fabrics that have come to light.}}
## Badge or sign of honour, rank, or official station.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1605 |title=The Tragedy of Coriolanus |author=William Shakespeare|page= |ISBN=|passage=In the official '''marks''' invested, you / Anon do meet the Senate.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} [[preeminence|Preeminence]]; high position.
##: {{uxa|en|patricians of '''mark'''}}
##: {{uxa|en|a fellow of no '''mark'''}}
## {{lb|en|logic}} A characteristic or essential [[attribute]]; a [[differential]].
## An indication or sign used for reference or measurement. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{uxa|en|I filled the bottle up to the 500 ml '''mark'''.}}
## {{lb|en|cooking}} A specified [[level]] on a scale denoting gas-powered oven [[temperatures]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
##: {{uxa|en|Now put the pastry in at 450 degrees, or '''mark''' 8.}}
## {{lb|en|nautical}} One of the bits of leather or coloured [[bunting]] placed upon a [[sound]]ing line at intervals of from two to five [[fathom]]s. (The unmarked fathoms are called "[[deep]]s".)
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[resemblance|Resemblance]], [[likeness]], [[image]]. {{defdate|14th–16th c.}}
## A particular design or make of an item {{qualifier|now usually with following numeral}}. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{uxa|en|I am proud to present my patented travelator, '''mark''' two.}}
## {{lb|en|product design/engineering}} The [[model number]] of a device; a device [[model]].
##: {{uxa|en|The '''Mark''' I system had poor radar, and the Mark II was too expensive; regardless, most antiaircraft direction remained the responsibility of the Mark I Eyeball (as the jocular phrase calls it): that is, the operator's eye.}}
# {{lb|en|heading}} ''Score, grade, or related judgement.''
## {{lb|en|Commonwealth}} A [[score]] for finding the correct answer, or other [[academic]] achievement; the sum of such points gained as out of a possible total. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
##: {{uxa|en|What '''mark''' did you get in your history test?}}
## A [[score]] for a [[sporting]] achievement. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
## An [[official]] [[note]] that is added to a [[record]] kept about someone's [[behavior]] or [[performance]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1871|author=Chicago Board of Education|title=Annual Report|volume=17|page=102
|passage=A '''mark''' for tardiness or for absence is considered by most pupils a disgrace, and strenuous efforts are made to avoid such a mark.}}
# {{lb|en|heading}} ''Target or indicator.''
## A [[target]] for [[shoot]]ing at with a projectile. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes}}, II.1:
##*: A skilfull archer ought first to know the '''marke''' he aimeth at, and then apply his hand, his bow, his string, his arrow and his motion accordingly.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1786|author=Francis Grose|title=A Treatise on Ancient Armour and Weapons|page=37
|passage=To give them an accurate eye and strength of arm, none under twenty-four years of age might shoot at any standing '''mark''', except it was for a rover, and then he was to change his '''mark''' at every shot; and no person above that age might shoot at any '''mark''' whose distance was less than eleven score yards.}}
## {{sid|en|victim}}{{lb|en|informal}} The [[target]] or intended [[victim]] of a [[swindle]], fixed game or [[con game]]; a [[gullible]] person. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Michael Benson|title=Cons and Frauds|year=2009|publisher=Infobase|page=21|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=B1mkWm6kRrUC&lpg=PA21&dq=%22short%20con%22&pg=PA21#v=onepage&q&f=false|isbn=978-1-4381-1659-4|passage=Another common form of short con is the shell game. This scam has the advantage of giving the criminal the ability to rip off many '''marks''' all at one location.}}
##* {{RQ:Bolton Room|313|[Xi Jinping] asked for Trump's personal attention to the issue, probably figuring he had identified his '''mark''' and wasn't going to let him get away.}}
##* {{quote-av|en|date=2022-12-11|writer={{w|Mike White (filmmaker)|Mike White}}|director=Mike White|episode=Arrivederci|title=w:The White Lotus|season=2|number=7|network=w:HBO|passage=Dominic Di Grasso ({{w|Michael Imperioli}}): How are you gonna make it in life if you're this big a '''mark'''?<br>Albie Di Grasso ({{w|Adam DiMarco}}): I'm not a '''mark'''.}}
## {{lb|en|professional wrestling}} A [[professional wrestling]] fan who believes matches are legitimate contests rather than [[scripted]] or predetermined.
##: {{antonym|en|smart}}
# {{lb|en|heading}} ''Attention.''
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1604 |title=Measure for Measure|author=William Shakespeare|page= |ISBN=|passage=But faults so countenanced, that the strong statutes Stand like the forfeits in a barber's shop, as much in mock as '''mark'''}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} [[attention|Attention]], [[notice]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{uxa|en|His last comment is particularly worthy of '''mark'''.}}
## [[importance|Importance]], [[noteworthiness]]. {{qualifier|Generally in postmodifier ''"of mark"''.}} {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1909|author=Richard Burton|title=Masters of the English Novel
|passage=in the short story of western flavor he was a pioneer of '''mark''', the founder of a genre: probably no other writer is so significant in his field.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} Regard; respect.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The female [[genitals]]. {{defdate|16th–18th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Love's Labour's Lost|I|4|text=A '''mark''' saies my Lady. Let the '''mark''' haue a prick in't, to meate at, if it may be.}}
#* {{RQ:Cleland Fanny Hill|text=her thighs were still spread, and the '''mark''' lay fair for him, who, now kneeling between them, displayed to us a side-view of that fierce erect machine of his{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|Rugby football, Australian rules football}} A catch of the ball directly from a kick of 10 metres or more without having been touched in transit, resulting in a [[free kick]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
# Limit or standard of action or fact.
#: {{uxa|en|to be within the '''mark'''}}
#: {{uxa|en|to come up to the '''mark'''}}

